INT. MOBILE DIALYSIS TRUCK - DAY

A converted delivery truck parked on a gravel lot beside a wheat field. Inside: three recliners, humming machines, tubing coiled like pale vines. Sunlight leaks through a sliver of window.

DR. THANDIWE MOLOI (40s, braids pinned up, lanyard heavy with keys) adjusts a monitor. ELLSWORTH CRANE (70s, farmer's cap, forearms like rope) sits in the nearest recliner, one arm hooked to a machine. NURSE KRZYSZTOF WIERZBICKI (30s, scrubs, a crossword folded in his pocket) calibrates a pump.

ELLSWORTH
You'd think a man could get his blood cleaned closer to home.

MOLOI
You'd think.

ELLSWORTH
Forty miles each way, three days a week.

KRZYSZTOF
It's thirty-eight miles, Mr. Crane.

ELLSWORTH
Not from my end.

MOLOI
How's the pressure?

ELLSWORTH
Same as always. Low and stubborn.

KRZYSZTOF
Hundred-ten over seventy.

MOLOI
Fine. Any dizziness?

ELLSWORTH
Only from the cooking at the diner.

KRZYSZTOF
Mr. Crane, you aren't supposed to be eating at the diner.

ELLSWORTH
Son, it's the only place that serves pie.

MOLOI
Ellsworth.

ELLSWORTH
One slice.

MOLOI
Last week it was three.

ELLSWORTH
It was a small week.

Krzysztof laughs into his mask. Moloi shakes her head, but she's smiling.

MOLOI
Okay. Run is two hours. If you need anything--

ELLSWORTH
I know. Press the button.

MOLOI
Press the button.

ELLSWORTH
And don't die.

MOLOI
That one too.

The machine hums. Ellsworth closes his eyes. Krzysztof returns to his crossword.

Then the machine chirps. A low, dull alarm.

Krzysztof and Moloi glance at the monitor at the same time. A red line climbs.

KRZYSZTOF
Air in the line.

MOLOI
Clamp it.

Krzysztof lunges. Moloi dives for the other end. Their hands collide on the tubing, fingers tangling, both clamp at once. The alarm shrieks.

ELLSWORTH
What's wrong?

MOLOI
Nothing. Stay still.

ELLSWORTH
Doctor.

MOLOI
Mr. Crane, I need you to breathe.

Krzysztof and Moloi lean over him, working in tandem. Krzysztof flushes the line. Moloi checks the needle. Ellsworth's face is gray.

Silence. The alarm stops. The monitor steadies. Ellsworth opens one eye.

ELLSWORTH
Was that me?

MOLOI
It was the line.

ELLSWORTH
Thank God.

KRZYSZTOF
(shaky)
Not funny, Mr. Crane.

ELLSWORTH
Wasn't joking.
